## Results
I couldn't build a validated mitigation plan. The instance and VPN in this incident can't be found in AWS account 012345678901_1, and the planning rules don't allow writing changes against resources whose current state I couldn't read. The fix itself is clear; it's laid out below so you can apply it once the resources are identified.

```json
{
  "mitigation_summary": {
    "action": "No mitigation action can be identified.",
    "reasoning": "There isn't enough system information for a complete, actionable plan for incident 'vpn-large-transfers-hang' in AWS account 012345678901_1. The cause is accepted as stated: a path MTU black hole over the Site-to-Site VPN. The tunnel carries at most 1446-byte packets (1406-byte TCP segments), or 1422/1382 with AES-CBC and NAT-T, and it doesn't support Path MTU Discovery. Flows that agree on a 1460-byte or larger segment size, with no clamping on the path, lose their full-size packets without any ICMP message coming back.\n\nWhat the read-only checks found:\n(1) No Site-to-Site VPN connections exist in any of the account's 17 enabled regions. The reported VPN is not an AWS-managed VPN connection in this account.\n(2) The instance ID 'i-0abc123def456789' is malformed (16 characters). A prefix search found no instance with that ID in us-east-1 or us-west-2.\n(3) us-east-1 has five customer gateway records: cgw-0123456789abc0004 'example-vpn-device-1' (54.214.118.147), cgw-0123456789abc0005 'example-vpn-device-3', cgw-0123456789abc0006, cgw-0123456789abc0007 and cgw-0123456789abc0008. No VPN connection uses any of them.\n(4) us-east-1 has virtual private gateways vgw-0123456789abc0004 'example-vpn-device-2' (attached to vpc-0123456789abc0002), vgw-0123456789abc0001 'example-name-wx' (attached to vpc-0123456789abc0003) and vgw-0123456789abc0005 'test' (not attached). It also has transit gateway tgw-0123456789abc0002, which has attachments to VPCs, a Connect attachment, two Direct Connect gateways and a peer, tgw-0123456789abc0004 in us-west-2. None of the transit gateway attachments is a VPN.\n\nPossible explanations: the tunnel belongs to a different AWS account; it is a self-managed IPsec tunnel (for example Openswan/strongSwan on EC2, a transit gateway Connect/SD-WAN appliance, or the 'example-vpn-device-1' customer gateway); or the traffic actually travels over Direct Connect. If the tunnel is self-managed, the limits quoted for AWS Site-to-Site VPN may not apply as stated, and the real tunnel MTU depends on that device. The on-premises customer gateway device and the file server are outside AWS and out of reach. Because of this, the 'this morning' trigger is still unconfirmed: removed MSS clamping, a tunnel failover, an encryption or NAT-T change, an instance MTU change, or a new instance.\n\nInformation needed before a safe plan can be built:\n(a) The correct 17-character instance ID and its region. Then run: aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids <id> --region <region>.\n(b) The account and resource that terminate the tunnel. If it's AWS-managed in another account: aws ec2 describe-vpn-connections --region <region> (check Options.TunnelOptions: Phase1/2EncryptionAlgorithms, and VgwTelemetry status, to spot this morning's failover). If it's self-managed, the device type and its IPsec settings.\n(c) Who controls the on-premises customer gateway device.\n\nOnce those are known, the documented fix, in order of preference, is:\n(1) Clamp TCP MSS to 1379 bytes on the customer gateway's tunnel interface (Cisco ASA: 'sysopt connection tcpmss 1379'; Cisco IOS: 'ip tcp adjust-mss 1379'; Linux/strongSwan: iptables -t mangle -A FORWARD -o <tunnel-if> -p tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN -j TCPMSS --set-mss 1379). 1379 is the value AWS recommends and stays under every algorithm limit, including 1382 for AES-CBC with NAT-T. Alternatively, fragment before encryption, or clear DF if the device supports it. Roll back by removing that one line. Change the standby tunnel's device too, so a later failover doesn't bring the problem back.\n(2) Host-side workaround on the EC2 instance (Linux), which only affects traffic to the on-premises range: sudo ip route add <onprem-CIDR> via <subnet-router-IP> dev <eth-if> mtu 1379 advmss 1339. Before running it, record 'ip route get <file-server-IP>' and 'ip link show <eth-if>'. Roll back with 'sudo ip route del <onprem-CIDR>'. The change doesn't survive a reboot until it's written into the OS network config, and an instance that doesn't use jumbo frames for other traffic could instead just set the interface MTU to 1379. On Windows, use netsh interface ipv4 set subinterface \"<name>\" mtu=1379 store=active.\n(3) Or lower the MTU on the file server.\n(4) Opening ICMP won't help, because PMTUD isn't supported over the tunnel.\n\nTo check the fix worked: repeat the large download, take a tshark capture and confirm the SYN/SYN-ACK show MSS ≤1379 and there are no repeated retransmissions of full-size segments. Also check CloudTrail (ModifyVpnTunnelOptions, ModifyVpnConnection, RunInstances, ModifyInstanceAttribute) for this morning's changes in the account and region that own the tunnel. Long-term: the clamp and MTU settings should be stored in the customer gateway's configuration management and in the instance's build or user data, so a replacement device or instance keeps them."
  }
}
```

